# Class: obo:FOODON_03412511 (hubbard squash plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412511 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412511 "Cucurbita maxima, one of at least four species of cultivated squash, is one of the most diverse domesticated species. This species originated in South America from the wild Cucurbita andreana over 4000 years ago. Hubbard squash is a cultivar of this species that usually has a tear-drop shape. They are often used as a replacement for pumpkins in cooking.[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cucurbita_maxima]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412511 <http://eol.org/pages/584406>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412511 obo:NCBITaxon_3661)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412511 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?12597>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412511 <http://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=22369>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412511 langual:B2511)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412511 "PLANTS:CUMA3")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412511 "Cucurbita maxima Duchesne")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412511 "Cucurbitaceae")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412511 "cucurbita maxima"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412511 "cucurbita maxima var. hubbard"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412511 "hubbard squash plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412511 obo:FOODON_03411189)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412519 (ground cherry plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412519 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412519 "Physalis is a genus of flowering plants in the nightshade family (*Solanaceae*), which grow in warm temperate and subtropical regions of the world. Most of the species, of which there may be 75-90, are indigenous to the New World. Cultivated species and weedy annuals have been introduced worldwide. A notable feature is the formation of a large papery husk derived from the calyx, which partly or fully encloses the fruit. The fruit is small and orange, similar in size, shape and structure to a small tomato.[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physalis]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412519 <http://eol.org/pages/4437>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412519 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?300461>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412519 langual:B2519)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412519 "PLANTS:PHYSA")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412519 "Physalis L.")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412519 "Physalis spp.")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412519 "groundcherry"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412519 "husk tomato"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412519 "physalis spp."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412519 "ground cherry plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412519 obo:FOODON_03411006)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412519 obo:FOODON_03414934)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412520 (tayberry plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412520 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412520 "A cross between red raspberry and other bramble berry species [The Plant Book, Cambridge University Press 1990, page 571]. Tayberry (*Rubus idaeus x fruticosus*) is a cultivated shrub in the genus *Rubus* of the family *Rosaceae* patented in 1979 as a cross between a loganberry and the black raspberry ."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03412520 "WIKIPEDIA:Tayberry")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412520 <http://eol.org/pages/8097>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412520 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?104968>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412520 langual:B2520)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412520 "Rubus loganobaccus L. H. Bailey")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03412520 "rubus idaeus x fructicosus"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03412520 "tayberry plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03412520 obo:FOODON_03411121)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03412523 (red kuri squash plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03412523 obo:IAO_0000122)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03412523 "Red kuri squash is thin skinned orange colored winter squash, that has the appearance of a small pumpkin without the ridges. It belongs to the Hubbard squash group. Inside the hard outer skin there is a firm flesh that provides a very delicate and mellow chestnut-like flavor.

Red kuri squash is commonly called \"Japanese squash\", \"orange Hokkaido squash\", \"baby red hubbard squash\", or \"Uchiki kuri squash\". In Japan, the word kuri may refer to either the squash discussed in this article or to Japanese chestnuts. In France, it is called potimarron, and in the United Kingdom, it is commonly called \"onion squash\".[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_kuri_squash]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412523 <http://eol.org/pages/584406>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412523 obo:NCBITaxon_3661)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412523 <http://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=22369>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03412523 langual:B2523)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412523 "Cucurbita maxima Duchesne")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412523 "Hokkaido squash"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412523 "red Hokkaido squash"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03412523 "sweet meat squash"@en)
