SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414302 obo:FOODON_03413357)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414303 (native rosella plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03414303 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414303 "Abelmoschus moschatus is a perennial species comprising two recognised subspecies. Subsp. *moschatus* occurs in India, parts of Asia and the Pacific Islands. There are also naturalised occurrences of subsp. *moschatus* in northern New South Wales. Subsp. *tuberosus* is found in northern Australia and in islands to the north. It is a delightful, soft, herbaceous trailing plant to 2 metres in diameter with soft hairy stems. It has an underground tuber and dies back to this tuber in the dry season, emerging again with the first substantial rains of the wet season. It is a relative of the edible okra and tubers and foliage were a source of food for Aborigines.[http://anpsa.org.au/a-mos.html]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414303 <http://eol.org/pages/4321>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414303 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?105540>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414303 langual:B4303)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03414303 "Abelmoschus moschatus subsp. tuberosus (Span.) Borss. Waalk.")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414303 "abelmoschus moschatus subsp. tuberosus"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03414303 "native rosella plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414303 obo:FOODON_03411058)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414303 obo:FOODON_03414282)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414306 (australian sunrise lime plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03414306 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414306 "A hybrid cross of Finger Lime and a calomondin which is pear shaped and orange inside.
The Australian Sunrise lime is a seedling of a Faustrimedin (originally bred in California in 1911), a hybrid of the Finger lime (Citrus australasica) and the Calamondin (Citrus × microcarpa), itself a hybrid between the Nagami kumquat (Citrus japonica 'Nagami') and the sour mandarin (Citrus sunki). Australian Sunrise produces attractive golden-coloured fruit on an upright shrub to small tree, usually 2 to 3 m high and 1.5 to 2.5 m wide. Foliage is dark, glossy-green. The oval leaves are approximately 40 to 45 mm long by 20 to 30 mm wide. 

 
The cream-coloured flowers occur in spring to early summer. Fruits ripen in winter, are pearshaped and usually 30 to 45 mm long by 20 to 40 mm wide. Seeds are small and plump.


Juice squeezed from the fruit has a sharp, clean flavour and a light \"floral\" aroma. The fruit may be eaten whole and like a kumquat, have a sharpish flesh and a sweet albedo and skin. They can be used in products such as in cordials, beverages, conserves, puree, pastes, sauces, marmalade, syrups and garnishes.[http://users.kymp.net/citruspages/australian.html#sunrise]"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03414306 "WIKIPEDIA:Australian_lime")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414306 <http://eol.org/pages/4414>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414306 langual:B4306)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414306 "citrus × oliveri"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03414306 "australian sunrise lime plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414306 obo:FOODON_03411067)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414307 (argyrosomus)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03414307 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414307 "Argyrosomus is a genus of fish in the drum family, *Sciaenidae*. They are large fish, with the largest, *Argyrosomus regius* growing up to 230 cm in length. They are commonly targeted as game fish."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03414307 "WIKIPEDIA:Argyrosomus")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414307 <http://eol.org/pages/23966>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414307 langual:B4307)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03414307 "argyrosomus"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414307 obo:FOODON_03412005)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414309 (grunter family)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03414309 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414309 "Grunters or tigerperches are fishes in the family *Terapontidae* (also spelled *Teraponidae*, *Theraponidae* or *Therapontidae*). They are found in shallow coastal waters in the Indian Ocean and western Pacific, where they live in saltwater, brackish and freshwater habitats. They grow up to 80 cm in length and feed on fishes, insects and other invertebrates.
When caught, grunters make the characteristic grunting sounds that give them their name."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03414309 "WIKIPEDIA:Terapontidae")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414309 langual:B4309)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414309 "grunters"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414309 "teraponidae"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414309 "terapontidae"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414309 "theraponidae"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414309 "therapontidae"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414309 "tigerperch"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03414309 "grunter family"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414309 obo:FOODON_03411581)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414311 (sillago-whiting family)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03414311 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414311 "The *Sillaginidae*, commonly known as the smelt-whitings, whitings, sillaginids, sand borers and sand-smelts, are a family of benthic coastal marine fishes in the order Perciformes. The smelt-whitings inhabit a wide region covering much of the Indo-Pacific, from the west coast of Africa east to Japan and south to Australia. The family comprises only three genera and thirty one species, of which a number are dubious, with the last major revision of the family in 1992 unable to confirm the validity of a number of species. They are elongate, slightly compressed fish often light brown to silver in colour with a variety of markings and patterns on their upper body. The Sillaginidae are not related to a number of fishes commonly called 'whiting' in the Northern Hemisphere, including the fish originally called whiting, *Merlangius merlangus*."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03414311 "WIKIPEDIA:Sillaginidae")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414311 <http://eol.org/pages/5204>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414311 langual:B4311)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "sand borers"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "sand-smelts"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "sillaginidae"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "slillaginids"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "smelt whitings"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "smelt-whitings"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414311 "whitings"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03414311 "sillago-whiting family"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414311 obo:FOODON_03411581)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414313 (blood orange plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:FOODON_03414313 obo:IAO_0000428)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414313 "The blood orange is a variety of orange (*Citrus sinensis*) with crimson, almost-blood-colored flesh. The fruit is smaller than an average orange; its skin is usually pitted, but can be smooth. The distinctive dark flesh color is due to the presence of anthocyanins, a family of pigments common to many flowers and fruit, but uncommon in citrus fruits. The flesh develops its characteristic maroon color when the fruit develops with low temperatures during the night. Sometimes there is dark coloring on the exterior of the rind as well, depending on the variety of blood orange. The skin can also be more tough and harder to peel than other oranges.  While all oranges are likely of hybrid origin between the pomelo and the tangerine, blood oranges originated as a mutation of the sweet orange. 
Within Europe, the Arancia Rossa di Sicilia (Red Orange of Sicily) has Protected Geographical Status."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03414313 "WIKIPEDIA:Blood_orange")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414313 <http://eol.org/pages/4414>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414313 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?10782>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414313 <http://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=28889>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414313 langual:B4313)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414313 "MANSFELD:7673")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414313 "PLANTS:CISI3")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03414313 "Citrus sinensis (L.) Osbeck")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:FOODON_03414313 "Citrus ×sinensis (L.) Osbeck (pro sp.)")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasSynonym obo:FOODON_03414313 "citrus sinensis"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:FOODON_03414313 "blood orange plant"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414313 obo:FOODON_03411339)

# Class: obo:FOODON_03414314 (yellow onion plant)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:FOODON_03414314 "The brown onion or yellow onion (*Allium cepa *L.) is a variety of dry onion with a strong flavour. They have a greenish-white, light yellow, or white inside, its layers of papery skin have a yellow-brown or pale golden colour. It is higher in sulphur content than the white onion, which gives it a stronger, more complex flavour."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:FOODON_03414314 wikipedia:Yellow_onion)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:FOODON_03414314 langual:B4314)
SubClassOf(obo:FOODON_03414314 obo:NCBITaxon_4679)

