# Class: obo:NCBITaxon_4538 (Oryza glaberrima)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000114 obo:NCBITaxon_4538 obo:IAO_0000122)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "A domesticated rice species commonly known as African rice."@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000118 obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "african rice plant"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000119 obo:NCBITaxon_4538 wikipedia:Oryza_glaberrima)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:NCBITaxon_4538 <http://eol.org/pages/1115583>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:NCBITaxon_4538 <http://www.ars-grin.gov/cgi-bin/npgs/html/taxon.pl?26062>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:NCBITaxon_4538 <http://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=506651>)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:NCBITaxon_4538 langual:B2512)
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasDbXref obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "PLANTS:ORGL2")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "Oryza glaberrima Steud.")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasNarrowSynonym obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "Oryza glaberrima Steudel")
AnnotationAssertion(go:hasRelatedSynonym obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "red rice"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:NCBITaxon_4538 "It was first domesticated and grown in West Africa, and was brought to the Americas by enslaved West African rice farmers. It is now largely a subsistence crop, rarely sold in markets even in West Africa. While it has been partly replaced by higher-yielding Asian rice, and the number of varieties grown is declining, it persists, making up an estimated 20% of rice grown in West Africa.

In comparison to Asian rice, it is hardy, pest-resistant, low-labour, suited to a variety of African conditions, filling, and has a distinct nutty flavour. 
Crossbreeding between African and Asian rice is difficult, but there exist some crosses."@en)
SubClassOf(obo:NCBITaxon_4538 obo:FOODON_03411322)

